      "standards": [
        "Plumber"
      ],
      "requirements": [
        "ISSUE-701"
      ],
      "status": "Fail",
      "status_id": 3,
      "status_details": [
        "job \"ci/build\" references action \"tj-actions/changed-files@v45.0.0\" with a mutable ref — pin by commit SHA instead"
      ]
    },
    "finding_info": {
      "uid": "plumber:getplumber-examples/plumber-cli-e2e:findings:<EPOCH>:actionsMustBePinnedByCommitSha",
      "title": "Third-party actions must be pinned by commit SHA",
      "desc": "A GitHub Actions workflow references a third-party action with a mutable ref (tag or branch) instead of a 40-character commit SHA. Tag and branch refs can be reassigned by the action's maintainer, or by an attacker who compromises the action's repository, to point at arbitrary code, which then runs inside the caller workflow with its secrets. The March 2025 tj-actions/changed-files compromise (CVE-2025-30066) propagated this way to hundreds of repositories, including aquasecurity/trivy. A tag backed by an immutable GitHub release (`immutable: true` on the release of that exact tag) can never be moved or deleted, so it is accepted as pinned and listed with `pinnedVia: immutable_release`; a floating major tag such as `@v4` has no release behind it and is still reported. The exemption needs a GitHub token: when the release cannot be read, the reference is reported. When the analyzed branch commits a workflow dependency lockfile (`.github/workflows/actions.lock`), the runner executes the commits it records for the repository's own steps, so those are accepted and listed with `pinnedVia: workflow_lockfile`. Reusable workflows are not in the lockfile schema: a reusable-workflow call is still reported when its ref is mutable, and so are the steps of a reusable workflow defined in another repository (that repository's own lockfile is not read). A caller's run fails to start when a called reusable workflow has no lockfile of its own, but that requirement does not pin the call. Plumber reads only the lockfile's presence: GitHub refuses to start any workflow the lockfile does not fully cover (not listed, stale or missing entry, malformed file).",
      "types": [
        "CI/CD Security",
        "Supply Chain",
        "Security"
      ],
      "src_url": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-701"
    },
    "remediation": {
      "desc": "Replace `uses: owner/action@v4` with `uses: owner/action@\u003c40-char-sha\u003e` and add a `# vX.Y.Z` comment to document the version, or reference the exact tag of an immutable release (`uses: owner/action@v4.2.1`) when the action publishes immutable releases. Tools like Dependabot's `version-update-strategy: sha-and-version` can automate the update flow. For step references, a workflow lockfile is an alternative (`gh extension install github/gh-actions-lock \u0026\u0026 gh actions-lock`); lockfiles are not available on GitHub Enterprise Server, and workflows using local-path actions cannot be onboarded yet. Reusable workflow calls still need a commit SHA, lockfile or not. Official GitHub-owned actions (actions/*, github/*) can be excluded if they are in the workflow's trusted boundary."
    },
    "resources": [
      {
        "type": "code_repository",
        "name": "getplumber-examples/plumber-cli-e2e",
        "uid": "https://github.com/getplumber-examples/plumber-cli-e2e",
