{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Plumber",
          "informationUri": "https://getplumber.io",
          "version": "dev",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-102",
              "name": "Forbidden container image tag",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Forbidden container image tag"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A container image in the pipeline uses a tag that is forbidden by the configuration (e.g., 'latest', 'dev'). Mutable tags make builds non-reproducible because the underlying image can change without notice."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-102 - Forbidden container image tag\n\nPin the image to a specific immutable version tag (e.g., 'python:3.12.1' instead of 'python:latest'). Configure forbidden tags in .plumber.yaml under containerImageMustNotUseForbiddenTags.tags.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-102",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-102** — Forbidden container image tag\n\nPin the image to a specific immutable version tag (e.g., 'python:3.12.1' instead of 'python:latest'). Configure forbidden tags in .plumber.yaml under containerImageMustNotUseForbiddenTags.tags.\n\n[View the ISSUE-102 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-102)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-102",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "containerImageMustNotUseForbiddenTags",
                "security-severity": "5.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-103",
              "name": "Container image is not pinned by digest",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Container image is not pinned by digest"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A container image in the pipeline is not pinned by its SHA256 digest. Without digest pinning, a tag can be reassigned to a different image, introducing supply chain risks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-103 - Container image is not pinned by digest\n\nPin the image using its digest: 'image: registry.example.com/myimage@sha256:abc123...'. You can find the digest with 'docker inspect --format={{.RepoDigests}} \u003cimage\u003e'.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-103",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-103** — Container image is not pinned by digest\n\nPin the image using its digest: 'image: registry.example.com/myimage@sha256:abc123...'. You can find the digest with 'docker inspect --format={{.RepoDigests}} \u003cimage\u003e'.\n\n[View the ISSUE-103 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-103)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-103",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "containerImageMustNotUseForbiddenTags",
                "security-severity": "5.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-203",
              "name": "Pipeline enables CI debug trace",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pipeline enables CI debug trace"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The pipeline has CI_DEBUG_TRACE or CI_DEBUG_SERVICES enabled, which exposes all secret variables in the job log output. This is a critical security risk in production pipelines."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-203 - Pipeline enables CI debug trace\n\nRemove or set CI_DEBUG_TRACE and CI_DEBUG_SERVICES to 'false' in your .gitlab-ci.yml variables section. These should only be used temporarily for debugging and never committed.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-203",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-203** — Pipeline enables CI debug trace\n\nRemove or set CI_DEBUG_TRACE and CI_DEBUG_SERVICES to 'false' in your .gitlab-ci.yml variables section. These should only be used temporarily for debugging and never committed.\n\n[View the ISSUE-203 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-203)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-203",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "pipelineMustNotEnableDebugTrace",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-207",
              "name": "Template injection in workflow script",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Template injection in workflow script"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions workflow inlines user-controlled template expressions (github.event.*, github.head_ref, ...) directly into a `run:` shell script. Attacker-controlled values such as PR titles, issue bodies or fork branch names can break out of the intended string and execute arbitrary commands with the job's secrets."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-207 - Template injection in workflow script\n\nMove the template expression into an `env:` binding first, then reference the environment variable from the shell (`\"$TITLE\"`). Shell expansion of a bound variable quotes the value and neutralises injection payloads.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-207",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-207** — Template injection in workflow script\n\nMove the template expression into an `env:` binding first, then reference the environment variable from the shell (`\"$TITLE\"`). Shell expansion of a bound variable quotes the value and neutralises injection payloads.\n\n[View the ISSUE-207 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-207)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-207",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "workflowMustNotInjectUserInputInScripts",
                "security-severity": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-209",
              "name": "Untrusted content written to $GITHUB_ENV or $GITHUB_PATH",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Untrusted content written to $GITHUB_ENV or $GITHUB_PATH"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions `run:` step appends a user-controlled value (PR title, issue body, fork branch name, ...) to `$GITHUB_ENV` or `$GITHUB_PATH`. The appended value becomes an environment variable or PATH entry for every subsequent step in the job. An attacker who controls the value can override existing variables (for example `NODE_OPTIONS=--require=./exfil.js`) or inject a malicious directory at the front of PATH, hijacking any later `npm`, `bash`, etc. invocation and exfiltrating secrets from privileged triggers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-209 - Untrusted content written to $GITHUB_ENV or $GITHUB_PATH\n\nDo not write user-controlled values into `$GITHUB_ENV` or `$GITHUB_PATH`. If you must propagate a value derived from user input, bind it through an `env:` block first (`env: { TITLE: ${{ github.event.issue.title }} }`) and validate/escape it before writing, or pass it as a step output instead. Restrict workflows touching these files to non-PR triggers when possible.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-209",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-209** — Untrusted content written to $GITHUB_ENV or $GITHUB_PATH\n\nDo not write user-controlled values into `$GITHUB_ENV` or `$GITHUB_PATH`. If you must propagate a value derived from user input, bind it through an `env:` block first (`env: { TITLE: ${{ github.event.issue.title }} }`) and validate/escape it before writing, or pass it as a step output instead. Restrict workflows touching these files to non-PR triggers when possible.\n\n[View the ISSUE-209 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-209)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-209",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "workflowMustNotWriteUntrustedContentToGitHubEnv",
                "security-severity": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-307",
              "name": "Checkout persists credentials in .git/config (latent)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Checkout persists credentials in .git/config (latent)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions job runs `actions/checkout` without disabling credential persistence. By default the action writes GITHUB_TOKEN into the cloned repository's .git/config, where it survives for the lifetime of the job. On its own this is latent hygiene: the token is discarded when the job ends. It becomes a demonstrable leak only when a later step packs `.git` into an uploaded artifact (escalated to ISSUE-310) or when fork-controlled code runs in the job (covered by ISSUE-802 / ISSUE-804)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-307 - Checkout persists credentials in .git/config (latent)\n\nAdd `with: { persist-credentials: false }` on every `uses: actions/checkout@*` step unless the job legitimately needs to push back. When push is required, scope the token with an explicit `permissions:` block.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-307",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-307** — Checkout persists credentials in .git/config (latent)\n\nAdd `with: { persist-credentials: false }` on every `uses: actions/checkout@*` step unless the job legitimately needs to push back. When push is required, scope the token with an explicit `permissions:` block.\n\n[View the ISSUE-307 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-307)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-307",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "checkoutMustNotPersistCredentials",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-309",
              "name": "Entire secrets context exported via toJson(secrets)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Entire secrets context exported via toJson(secrets)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions workflow serialises the whole `secrets` context with `toJson(secrets)` or `toJSON(secrets)` and pipes the result into a step's environment, run script, or `with:` input. The resulting string contains every secret the job has access to (repository, organisation and environment) and travels through whatever downstream consumer the step passes it to (a third-party action, a remote server, a log line). Even with GitHub's automatic log redaction, a single `echo` of the JSON payload has been enough to leak tokens in past supply-chain incidents; a compromised reusable action sees them directly regardless of logging."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-309 - Entire secrets context exported via toJson(secrets)\n\nPass only the specific secrets the step needs, by name: `env: { TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }} }`. If the step forwards credentials to a reusable workflow, name each one in the `secrets:` block of the call rather than using `toJson(secrets)`.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-309",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-309** — Entire secrets context exported via toJson(secrets)\n\nPass only the specific secrets the step needs, by name: `env: { TOKEN: ${{ secrets.NPM_TOKEN }} }`. If the step forwards credentials to a reusable workflow, name each one in the `secrets:` block of the call rather than using `toJson(secrets)`.\n\n[View the ISSUE-309 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-309)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-309",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "workflowMustNotExportEntireSecretsContext",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-410",
              "name": "Security job weakened",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Security job weakened"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A security job in the pipeline has been weakened by setting allow_failure to true, overriding rules with when: never or when: manual, or setting when to manual. This can cause critical security scans to be skipped or require manual intervention."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-410 - Security job weakened\n\nEnsure security jobs run automatically and block the pipeline on failure. Remove allow_failure: true, do not override rules with when: never or when: manual, and do not set when: manual on security jobs.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-410",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-410** — Security job weakened\n\nEnsure security jobs run automatically and block the pipeline on failure. Remove allow_failure: true, do not override rules with when: never or when: manual, and do not set when: manual on security jobs.\n\n[View the ISSUE-410 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-410)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-410",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "securityJobsMustNotBeWeakened",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-411",
              "name": "Unverified script execution",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unverified script execution"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A CI/CD job executes a script without integrity verification: pipe-to-shell (curl | bash), download-then-exec, redirect-then-exec, base64-decode-to-shell (Megalodon-style), or any command piped into bash/sh. An attacker can exfiltrate CI secrets via a compromised URL or obfuscated inline payload."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-411 - Unverified script execution\n\nDownload scripts to a file, verify checksum or signature on the same line, then execute. Avoid piping remote or encoded content directly into a shell. Vendor scripts in-repo or use trusted package managers.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-411",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-411** — Unverified script execution\n\nDownload scripts to a file, verify checksum or signature on the same line, then execute. Avoid piping remote or encoded content directly into a shell. Vendor scripts in-repo or use trusted package managers.\n\n[View the ISSUE-411 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-411)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-411",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "pipelineMustNotExecuteUnverifiedScripts",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-701",
              "name": "Third-party action reference is not pinned by commit SHA",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Third-party action reference is not pinned by commit SHA"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions workflow references a third-party action with a mutable ref (tag or branch) instead of a 40-character commit SHA. Tag and branch refs can be reassigned by the action's maintainer, or by an attacker who compromises the action's repository, to point at arbitrary code, which then runs inside the caller workflow with its secrets. The March 2025 tj-actions/changed-files compromise (CVE-2025-30066) propagated this way to hundreds of repositories, including aquasecurity/trivy. A tag backed by an immutable GitHub release (`immutable: true` on the release of that exact tag) can never be moved or deleted, so it is accepted as pinned and listed with `pinnedVia: immutable_release`; a floating major tag such as `@v4` has no release behind it and is still reported. The exemption needs a GitHub token: when the release cannot be read, the reference is reported. When the analyzed branch commits a workflow dependency lockfile (`.github/workflows/actions.lock`), the runner executes the commits it records for the repository's own steps, so those are accepted and listed with `pinnedVia: workflow_lockfile`. Reusable workflows are not in the lockfile schema: a reusable-workflow call is still reported when its ref is mutable, and so are the steps of a reusable workflow defined in another repository (that repository's own lockfile is not read). A caller's run fails to start when a called reusable workflow has no lockfile of its own, but that requirement does not pin the call. Plumber reads only the lockfile's presence: GitHub refuses to start any workflow the lockfile does not fully cover (not listed, stale or missing entry, malformed file)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-701 - Third-party action reference is not pinned by commit SHA\n\nReplace `uses: owner/action@v4` with `uses: owner/action@\u003c40-char-sha\u003e` and add a `# vX.Y.Z` comment to document the version, or reference the exact tag of an immutable release (`uses: owner/action@v4.2.1`) when the action publishes immutable releases. Tools like Dependabot's `version-update-strategy: sha-and-version` can automate the update flow. For step references, a workflow lockfile is an alternative (`gh extension install github/gh-actions-lock \u0026\u0026 gh actions-lock`); lockfiles are not available on GitHub Enterprise Server, and workflows using local-path actions cannot be onboarded yet. Reusable workflow calls still need a commit SHA, lockfile or not. Official GitHub-owned actions (actions/*, github/*) can be excluded if they are in the workflow's trusted boundary.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-701",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-701** — Third-party action reference is not pinned by commit SHA\n\nReplace `uses: owner/action@v4` with `uses: owner/action@\u003c40-char-sha\u003e` and add a `# vX.Y.Z` comment to document the version, or reference the exact tag of an immutable release (`uses: owner/action@v4.2.1`) when the action publishes immutable releases. Tools like Dependabot's `version-update-strategy: sha-and-version` can automate the update flow. For step references, a workflow lockfile is an alternative (`gh extension install github/gh-actions-lock \u0026\u0026 gh actions-lock`); lockfiles are not available on GitHub Enterprise Server, and workflows using local-path actions cannot be onboarded yet. Reusable workflow calls still need a commit SHA, lockfile or not. Official GitHub-owned actions (actions/*, github/*) can be excluded if they are in the workflow's trusted boundary.\n\n[View the ISSUE-701 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-701)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-701",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "actionsMustBePinnedByCommitSha",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-702",
              "name": "Action is hosted in an archived repository",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Action is hosted in an archived repository"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions workflow references an action whose upstream repository is archived. Archived repos no longer receive maintenance (security fixes, dependency bumps, compatibility patches all stop), and any existing vulnerability stays open forever. A new SHA can still be pushed by the last maintainer, so even pinning by SHA does not make the reference safe against a future takeover."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-702 - Action is hosted in an archived repository\n\nReplace the action with a maintained fork (audit the fork's owner first) or with an equivalent step implemented inline. If the behaviour is trivial, inlining the shell logic avoids the supply-chain dependency entirely.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-702",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-702** — Action is hosted in an archived repository\n\nReplace the action with a maintained fork (audit the fork's owner first) or with an equivalent step implemented inline. If the behaviour is trivial, inlining the shell logic avoids the supply-chain dependency entirely.\n\n[View the ISSUE-702 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-702)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-702",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "actionsMustNotBeArchived",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-703",
              "name": "Action version carries a published security advisory",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Action version carries a published security advisory"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The pinned version of this action appears in the GitHub Advisory Database under the `actions` ecosystem. Running a workflow on a known-vulnerable action version inherits the published vulnerability class (RCE, secret exfiltration, privilege escalation, depending on the advisory). Examples from history: tj-actions/changed-files (CVE-2025-30066), reviewdog/action-setup with the March 2025 compromise, unpatched releases of `actions/artifact`. This check reports every advisory whose affected-version range covers the pinned ref."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-703 - Action version carries a published security advisory\n\nUpgrade the action to a version outside the advisory's affected range (the advisory page lists a fixed-in version). Pin by SHA once upgraded so future retags cannot silently revert the fix. Configure Dependabot with `package-ecosystem: github-actions` to receive PR alerts when new advisories land.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-703",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-703** — Action version carries a published security advisory\n\nUpgrade the action to a version outside the advisory's affected range (the advisory page lists a fixed-in version). Pin by SHA once upgraded so future retags cannot silently revert the fix. Configure Dependabot with `package-ecosystem: github-actions` to receive PR alerts when new advisories land.\n\n[View the ISSUE-703 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-703)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-703",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "actionsMustNotCarryKnownCVEs",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-707",
              "name": "Pinned commit SHA does not belong to the action's repository",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Pinned commit SHA does not belong to the action's repository"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A workflow pins an action to a commit SHA that does not resolve in the action's upstream repository. That means either the SHA was mistyped (the reference silently runs whatever tag/branch GitHub falls back to, typically the default branch) or, far worse, the author was tricked by a PR comment / stargazer dashboard showing a detached commit that was never merged upstream. The name-and-SHA combination is the precise form of the `impostor commit` attack documented in academic supply-chain literature."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-707 - Pinned commit SHA does not belong to the action's repository\n\nReplace the reference with a SHA that actually belongs to the action's repository. Use `gh api repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/commits/\u003csha\u003e` to verify before committing the change. That endpoint only accepts commit SHAs, so a pin naming an annotated tag object answers 422 there; check it with `gh api repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/git/tags/\u003csha\u003e` instead, which also reports the tag name and the commit it points at.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-707",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-707** — Pinned commit SHA does not belong to the action's repository\n\nReplace the reference with a SHA that actually belongs to the action's repository. Use `gh api repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/commits/\u003csha\u003e` to verify before committing the change. That endpoint only accepts commit SHAs, so a pin naming an annotated tag object answers 422 there; check it with `gh api repos/\u003cowner\u003e/\u003crepo\u003e/git/tags/\u003csha\u003e` instead, which also reports the tag name and the commit it points at.\n\n[View the ISSUE-707 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-707)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-707",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "actionRefsMustExistUpstream",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-713",
              "name": "Action comes from an unauthorized source",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Action comes from an unauthorized source"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions workflow references an action whose source is not authorized. Every third-party action runs with the caller workflow's token and secrets, so an unvetted owner is a direct supply-chain entry point: the vector behind the tj-actions/changed-files compromise (CVE-2025-30066), where an attacker also exploited repository rename/re-creation to squat a once-trusted name. Restricting actions to GitHub-official owners, an explicit org allowlist, and a minimum-popularity floor shrinks that attack surface to vetted sources."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-713 - Action comes from an unauthorized source\n\nUse an action from an authorized source. Configure the allowlist in .plumber.yaml under githubActionMustComeFromAuthorizedSources: keep trustGithubOfficialActions on for actions/* and github/*, add trusted owners or actions to trustedGithubActions (exact `owner/repo` or `owner/*`), and optionally set minimumStars to require a popularity threshold. Vendoring the action into a local `./.github/actions/…` directory removes the external dependency entirely.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-713",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-713** — Action comes from an unauthorized source\n\nUse an action from an authorized source. Configure the allowlist in .plumber.yaml under githubActionMustComeFromAuthorizedSources: keep trustGithubOfficialActions on for actions/* and github/*, add trusted owners or actions to trustedGithubActions (exact `owner/repo` or `owner/*`), and optionally set minimumStars to require a popularity threshold. Vendoring the action into a local `./.github/actions/…` directory removes the external dependency entirely.\n\n[View the ISSUE-713 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-713)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-713",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "githubActionMustComeFromAuthorizedSources",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-802",
              "name": "Dangerous workflow trigger",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Dangerous workflow trigger"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions job is reachable via a trigger that runs with the base repository's secrets while being influenceable by an unprivileged caller (`workflow_run`, `issue_comment`, `pull_request_review`, `pull_request_review_comment`, `discussion`, `discussion_comment`, `gollum`, `fork`) AND it checks out fork-controlled code. The `pull_request_target` case is owned by ISSUE-804 (pull-request-target-with-head-checkout). This is the same exploit class as the March 2025 tj-actions/changed-files supply-chain compromise (CVE-2025-30066)."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-802 - Dangerous workflow trigger\n\nPrefer the standard `pull_request` trigger unless access to base-repo secrets is strictly required. If a secret-bearing trigger is necessary, never check out fork content and never render github.event.* / github.head_ref into shell commands. For `workflow_run`, keep the job restricted to non-secret-bearing steps.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-802",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-802** — Dangerous workflow trigger\n\nPrefer the standard `pull_request` trigger unless access to base-repo secrets is strictly required. If a secret-bearing trigger is necessary, never check out fork content and never render github.event.* / github.head_ref into shell commands. For `workflow_run`, keep the job restricted to non-secret-bearing steps.\n\n[View the ISSUE-802 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-802)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-802",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "workflowMustNotUseDangerousTriggers",
                "security-severity": "2.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-803",
              "name": "Excessive workflow permissions",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Excessive workflow permissions"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A GitHub Actions job's effective permissions block grants `write-all`, giving its GITHUB_TOKEN write access to every API scope regardless of what the job actually needs. Paired with a compromise through injection, dangerous triggers or a malicious action, this amplifies impact to full repository control."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-803 - Excessive workflow permissions\n\nDeclare the narrowest permissions block that lets the job do its work. Prefer `permissions: { contents: read }` at the workflow level and grant additional scopes only on the jobs that truly need them.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-803",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-803** — Excessive workflow permissions\n\nDeclare the narrowest permissions block that lets the job do its work. Prefer `permissions: { contents: read }` at the workflow level and grant additional scopes only on the jobs that truly need them.\n\n[View the ISSUE-803 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-803)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-803",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "workflowMustNotGrantPermissionsWriteAll",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ISSUE-804",
              "name": "pull_request_target workflow explicitly checks out the PR head",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "pull_request_target workflow explicitly checks out the PR head"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A workflow triggered by `pull_request_target` calls `actions/checkout` with an explicit `ref:` pointing at the pull request's head (e.g. `github.event.pull_request.head.sha`, `github.head_ref`). This is the exact pattern behind the March 2025 tj-actions/changed-files compromise (CVE-2025-30066): the workflow has access to the base repository's secrets AND it executes code controlled by the PR author. Any shell step that runs after the checkout is a direct path to secret exfiltration. Unlike the broader dangerous-triggers check (ISSUE-802) which flags the trigger itself, this rule flags the actual exploitable combination."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "ISSUE-804 - pull_request_target workflow explicitly checks out the PR head\n\nEither switch to the standard `pull_request` event (runs in the fork's context, no base-repo secrets), or remove the explicit `ref:` input so `actions/checkout` falls back to the base repository's SHA. If cross-context code must be examined, split the job: a small pull_request_target job gathers metadata, then hands off to a separate pull_request workflow that executes the fork code.\n\nhttps://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-804",
                "markdown": "**ISSUE-804** — pull_request_target workflow explicitly checks out the PR head\n\nEither switch to the standard `pull_request` event (runs in the fork's context, no base-repo secrets), or remove the explicit `ref:` input so `actions/checkout` falls back to the base repository's SHA. If cross-context code must be examined, split the job: a small pull_request_target job gathers metadata, then hands off to a separate pull_request workflow that executes the fork code.\n\n[View the ISSUE-804 documentation](https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-804)"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://getplumber.io/docs/cli/issues/ISSUE-804",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "controlName": "pullRequestTargetMustNotCheckoutHead",
                "security-severity": "9.5"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "results": [
